WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.821 --> 00:00:13.482
Welcome back, Little Fox Readers.

2
00:00:14.382 --> 00:00:21.387
I was really excited about this week's landmark because it's in London, England—the city I'll be visiting this

3
00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:22.687
summer with my family!

4
00:00:23.568 --> 00:00:29.172
Mr. Fay taught us all about the Tower of London, one of the most famous castles in the

5
00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:37.450
world. The Tower of London started out as just one tall, square-shaped fortress known as the White Tower,

6
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.911
which was built almost a thousand years ago.

7
00:00:41.911 --> 00:00:48.773
Over time British kings expanded this fortress by constructing more towers and walls around the White Tower.

8
00:00:49.694 --> 00:00:57.196
Today this entire complex of buildings and walls is known as the Tower of London, or just the

9
00:00:57.296 --> 00:01:02.146
Tower. Medieval kings used the Tower as a royal residence.

10
00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:09.010
They also stored weapons and valuables there, along with exotic animals like lions and bears.

11
00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:13.953
Sometimes these animals were allowed to wander around outside.

12
00:01:14.854 --> 00:01:20.957
King Henry III's polar bear, for example, was put on a long chain so it could go fishing

13
00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:21.918
in the Thames River.

14
00:01:21.938 --> 00:01:25.280
I bet the locals were pretty terrified!

15
00:01:26.204 --> 00:01:29.686
Around 1100 the Tower began being used as a prison.

16
00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:37.752
The first prisoner to be kept there was Bishop Ranulf Flambard, a tax collector who found a clever

17
00:01:37.792 --> 00:01:38.612
way to escape.

18
00:01:40.113 --> 00:01:44.616
Flambard had some friends deliver a gallon of wine with a rope hidden inside.

19
00:01:45.557 --> 00:01:50.660
On the day of a special feast, he invited his guards to celebrate and have some wine.

20
00:01:51.621 --> 00:01:55.804
Then when the guards fell asleep, he used the rope to climb out the window!

21
00:01:56.692 --> 00:02:01.079
For many centuries after that, the Tower continued serving as a prison.

22
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:08.810
Mr. Fay told us stories about some of the most famous and interesting prisoners, like Queen Anne Boleyn,

23
00:02:09.331 --> 00:02:15.638
who was beheaded for failing to give birth to a son; Hew Draper, who was accused of practicing

24
00:02:15.698 --> 00:02:24.224
sorcery and left an intricate astrological carving on his cell wall; and Guy Fawkes, an explosives expert who

25
00:02:24.284 --> 00:02:28.648
tried to blow up the entire ruling class, including the royal family!

26
00:02:30.189 --> 00:02:33.091
One story was very sad—and scary.

27
00:02:33.871 --> 00:02:38.915
Prince Edward V and his younger brother, Richard, were sent to the Tower in 1483.

28
00:02:40.672 --> 00:02:42.833
They were only twelve and ten years old.

29
00:02:43.754 --> 00:02:50.218
Their uncle, the Duke of Gloucester, had imprisoned the princes so that they wouldn't interfere with his becoming

30
00:02:50.278 --> 00:02:56.562
king. And here's the really spooky part: the boys were never seen again.

31
00:02:57.623 --> 00:03:05.448
Most people think they were murdered because many years later, two child-sized skeletons were discovered near the White

32
00:03:05.508 --> 00:03:12.939
Tower. By the 1800s the Tower wasn't being used much as a prison or royal residence anymore.

33
00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:19.562
An architect named Anthony Salvin was hired to restore it to its medieval appearance and make it more

34
00:03:19.622 --> 00:03:20.782
suitable for tourists.

35
00:03:22.523 --> 00:03:30.087
Salvin renovated buildings and rooms, making it easier to see graffiti written by prisoners on cell walls and

36
00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:36.921
creating a place to showcase the crown jewels, a collection of precious ornaments worn by British royalty.

37
00:03:37.921 --> 00:03:41.862
After these renovations the number of tourists at the Tower grew quickly.

38
00:03:42.703 --> 00:03:49.324
By 1901 over half a million people were visiting each year, and today that number is close to

39
00:03:49.424 --> 00:03:50.185
two million.

40
00:03:51.145 --> 00:03:56.546
When my family and I go to London, we're definitely going to spend at least a day there.

41
00:03:57.487 --> 00:04:02.028
My dad and little brother want to see the royal armor that's on display in the White Tower.

42
00:04:03.496 --> 00:04:08.879
According to my dad, some of the armor was worn by kings five hundred years ago!

43
00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:15.303
The royal armor sounds cool, but my first stop is definitely going to be the Jewel House, where

44
00:04:15.323 --> 00:04:16.603
the crown jewels are kept.

45
00:04:17.564 --> 00:04:23.907
Mr. Fay said that there are more than 23,000 gems in the collection, mostly found on scepters and

46
00:04:23.967 --> 00:04:30.951
crowns. One of the most famous items is the Imperial State Crown, which is decorated with over three

47
00:04:31.051 --> 00:04:37.095
thousand gems, including diamonds, pearls, rubies, and sapphires.

48
00:04:38.115 --> 00:04:44.459
The royals still wear some of these crowns, but I won't be able to try anything on: security

49
00:04:44.539 --> 00:04:45.440
is very tight!

50
00:04:46.901 --> 00:04:51.864
I also want to see the yeoman warders—soldiers who are part of the Royal Bodyguard.

51
00:04:52.804 --> 00:04:58.127
According to legend these soldiers were once allowed to eat as much beef as they wanted from the

52
00:04:58.187 --> 00:04:58.888
king's table.

53
00:04:59.888 --> 00:05:02.791
And so they became known as beefeaters.

54
00:05:03.891 --> 00:05:07.154
Many visitors go on tours given by the yeoman warders.

55
00:05:08.055 --> 00:05:15.921
The yeoman warders relate the history of the Tower, including gory stories of torture and executions of prisoners.

56
00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:21.986
They even tell stories about ghosts that supposedly haunt the Tower to this day!

57
00:05:22.886 --> 00:05:27.430
The yeoman warders and their families actually get to live in the Tower.

58
00:05:28.331 --> 00:05:29.612
Pretty awesome, huh?

59
00:05:30.448 --> 00:05:35.652
And they share their home with some very important creatures: the Tower ravens.

60
00:05:37.153 --> 00:05:43.797
In the seventeenth century, King Charles II decreed that there must always be at least six ravens living

61
00:05:43.837 --> 00:05:44.438
at the Tower.

62
00:05:45.458 --> 00:05:51.302
Legend says that if the ravens ever leave the Tower, both the Tower and the kingdom of England

63
00:05:51.342 --> 00:05:52.083
will fall!

64
00:05:53.103 --> 00:05:58.467
In recent years there have been six ravens living at the Tower, along with at least one or

65
00:05:58.527 --> 00:05:59.648
two extra birds.

66
00:06:00.588 --> 00:06:07.451
The Ravenmaster, a special yeoman warder, cares for these birds, who are very intelligent and can live for

67
00:06:07.511 --> 00:06:08.192
many years.

68
00:06:09.172 --> 00:06:14.395
Their wings are clipped so that they can't fly too far away, and visitors often spot them on

69
00:06:14.415 --> 00:06:14.995
the grounds.

70
00:06:16.056 --> 00:06:18.657
I hope I get to see a few when I visit the Tower!

71
00:06:20.057 --> 00:06:24.960
Last night I had to babysit my brother, and we pretended we were living in the Tower.

72
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.603
I made a crown and scepter for myself and pretended to be the queen of England.

73
00:06:31.603 --> 00:06:35.505
My brother made a sword and some armor and pretended to be a knight.

74
00:06:36.526 --> 00:06:43.430
He rescued me from an evil dragon—the vacuum cleaner—and bravely battled it until Mom and Dad got home.

75
00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.252
I really liked learning about the Tower of London in Mr.

76
00:06:47.292 --> 00:06:48.052
Fay's class.

77
00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:51.874
Now I really can't wait until summertime.

78
00:06:52.815 --> 00:06:54.496
London, here I come!
